# tasks.topic -- task-oriented entry points: what to write to do a common
# thing, found by the words people search with ("print output", "handle
# several errors"). Each answers the task and points at the topics behind it.

@topic task.print-output
title: Printing output
summary: std::io::print_line(text) prints a line on the native backends and in executables, with std::linux::io's context installed; on every backend the program's value is printed for you.
index: stdlib backends
alias: print output; print; println; print a line; write to stdout; hello world; console output
keywords: print, output, stdout, stderr, io, hello, print_line, write
related: backend.io stdlib.std::io::print_line stdlib.module.std::io concept.context-traits concept.program-value backend.native-only task.number-text
docs: CONTEXT-TRAITS.md

== THE PROGRAM
    import std::io
    import std::linux::io

    fn hello() errors std::io::WriteFailed:
        std::io::print_line("Hello, world!")

    with context std::linux::io::create()
    hello():
        on std::io::WriteFailed:
            unit

  * `import std::io` for the portable operations (print, print_line,
    write_error, write_error_line); `import std::linux::io` for the context
    that implements them.
  * `with context std::linux::io::create()` installs it, at the entry
    program's top level, before the first call that prints
    (concept.contexts).
  * Printing can fail (std::io::WriteFailed): handle it or admit it.

@example accept
import std::io
import std::linux::io

fn hello() errors std::io::WriteFailed:
    std::io::print_line("Hello, world!")

with context std::linux::io::create()
hello():
    on std::io::WriteFailed:
        unit
@output Hello, world!
@value unit
@backends cranelift-generic cranelift
@end

== WHERE IT WORKS
Native backends (-backend cranelift or cranelift-generic) and standalone
executables, on Linux: std::linux::io makes raw system calls, which interp
and compile refuse at run time (NATIVE-ONLY; backend.io). On macOS install
std::darwin::io's context instead, on Windows std::windows::io's
(examples/io/hello-windows.bot): hello() stays the same. For output on every
backend, make the text the program's value: the compiler prints it after
"value:", and an executable prints it last.

== NUMBERS
Convert a number to text first: std::int::to_string(n) (import std::int),
std::decimal_rounded::to_string(x) for a DecimalRounded value
(task.number-text).

@topic task.handle-several-errors
title: Handling several declared errors
summary: Write one `on NAME:` handler per error a call may raise (their values may be equal); there is no `on A, B:`; a callee can instead declare one error whose payload says which failure.
index: concepts rules
alias: handle several errors; handle multiple errors; catch multiple errors; several handlers; catch multiple exceptions; handle multiple exceptions
keywords: handler, errors, several, multiple, on, payload, unhandled
related: rule.one-handler-per-error capability.multi-error-handler concept.errors concept.error-payloads rule.unhandled-errors error.unhandled-error warning.same-return-value
docs: EXPLICIT-ERROR-COMPLETIONS.md ERROR-PAYLOADS.md DIAGNOSTIC-RECOVERY.md

== ONE HANDLER PER ERROR
    moves = solve(board, 40):
        on Unsolvable:
            []
        on LimitExceeded:
            []
The compiler reports every error a call may raise that is neither handled
nor admitted, in one diagnostic ("may produce the declared errors "A", "B"
and "C""), so you see the whole set at once. A handler's value is the
call's value; `return` or `fail` in a handler leaves the function.
Handlers that end the same way are not flagged by SAME-RETURN-VALUE or
SAME-FAILURE: the language requires one handler per error.

@example accept
error Unsolvable
error LimitExceeded

fn solve(n: int) -> List[int] errors Unsolvable, LimitExceeded:
    if n < 0:
        fail Unsolvable
    if n > 40:
        fail LimitExceeded
    [n]

fn solution(n: int) -> List[int]:
    moves = solve(n):
        on Unsolvable:
            []
        on LimitExceeded:
            []
    moves

solution(50)
@value []
@quiet
@end

== ONE ERROR WITH A PAYLOAD
When every caller handles a family of failures alike, the callee's author
can declare one error that says which failure it was:
    enum Problem:
        Empty, Duplicate, OutOfRange
    error InvalidBoard:
        problem: Problem
    ...
    fail InvalidBoard {problem: Problem::Duplicate}
Callers write one handler (`on InvalidBoard {problem}:`) and can still look
at the reason.

== PASSING THEM ON
Admit some or all in the enclosing function's own `errors` clause; its
callers then decide.

@topic task.string-concatenation
title: Joining text
summary: std::str::concat(a, b) (written a.concat(b) after `import std::str`) joins two Strings, std::str::append_char(s, c) appends a character; `+` is for Ints only.
index: stdlib
alias: string concatenation; concatenate strings; join strings; append string; string plus
keywords: concat, join, append, String, text, plus
related: stdlib.std::str::concat stdlib.std::str::append_char concept.strings capability.string-formatting task.number-text stdlib.std::test::joined

== HOW
    import std::str
    greeting = "Hello, ".concat(name)
    line = greeting.append_char('!')
`+` adds Ints; applied to Strings it fails (a TYPE error). For many parts,
chain concat calls or use stdlib/test.bot's std::test::joined(parts,
separator). Numbers need std::int::to_string first (task.number-text).

@example accept
import std::str

fn greet(name: str) -> str:
    "Hello, ".concat(name).append_char('!')

greet("Botlish")
@value "Hello, Botlish!"
@quiet
@end

@topic task.iterate-with-index
title: Iterating with an index
summary: Use a lockstep loop, `loop x in xs and i from 0 to xs.length():`, to get each element and its position; the compiler proves the two clauses run equally often.
index: concepts
alias: iterate with index; enumerate; loop with index; for each with index
keywords: index, enumerate, loop, lockstep, position, element
related: concept.lockstep concept.loops proof.lockstep proof.list-bounds

== HOW
    loop x in xs and i from 0 to xs.length():
        ...x and i...
There is no enumerate function: lockstep composes any equal-length
clauses, and the compiler proves they are equal (proof.lockstep). For the
index alone, `loop i from 0 to xs.length():` with xs.at(i) is proven in
range (proof.list-bounds).

@example accept
import std::list

fn numbered(xs: List[str]) -> List[int]:
    loop x in xs and i from 0 to xs.length():
        i * 10

numbered(["one", "two", "three"])
@value [0, 10, 20]
@end

@topic task.choose-backend
title: Choosing a backend
summary: Use the native backends (cranelift) or an executable for speed and for printing, compile when only Tcl is available, interp as the reference and for small checks.
index: backends
alias: choose backend; which backend should I use; select backend; fastest backend
keywords: backend, choose, speed, print, native, interp, compile
related: backend.overview backend.performance backend.io backend.native-executable cli.options

== IN SHORT
    develop and run          -backend cranelift (prints output, fast)
    no native build          -backend compile
    the reference semantics  -backend interp (default; slowest)
    ship a program           -emit-native-executable
All four accept and reject the same programs; tests of portable code
(golden suites) usually run on all four (backend.overview).

@topic task.read-arguments
title: Reading command-line arguments
summary: argv() returns the process arguments as Strings, argument zero first (InvalidArgumentEncoding for non-UTF-8); botlish passes them with -argv, an executable reads its own.
index: stdlib
alias: command line arguments; program arguments; read arguments; args
keywords: argv, arguments, command line, process
related: concept.argv stdlib.argv cli.options backend.native-executable
docs: ARGV.md

== HOW
    args = argv():
        on InvalidArgumentEncoding:
            []
Run by botlish, a program's arguments are those of -argv TEXT (repeatable;
the first is argument zero), -argv-hex HEX or -argv-none; without them
argv() is ["botlish-runner"]. A standalone executable reads its real process
arguments each time it runs.

@example accept
args = argv():
    on InvalidArgumentEncoding:
        []
args
@value ["botlish-runner"]
@end

@topic task.list-element
title: Reading an element of a list
summary: xs.at(i) (std::list::at, after `import std::list`) returns the element or fails with IndexNotFound; xs.get(i, default) returns a default; `loop x in xs:` visits every element.
index: stdlib
alias: list element; list index access; get element; nth element; access list item; xs[i]; xs[0]; subscript; index operator; square brackets
keywords: list, element, index, at, get, first, last, subscript, brackets
related: proof.list-bounds stdlib.std::list::at stdlib.std::list::get concept.lists task.iterate-with-index
match: SURFACE SYNTAX | expected a type after "*\[", found*

== HOW
    import std::list
    first = xs.at(0):                  # handle the missing element...
        on IndexNotFound:
            0
    first = xs.get(0, 0)               # ...or name a default
Inside a counted loop over xs.length(), or after a guard on the index,
`xs.at(i)` needs no handler (proof.list-bounds lists the proven forms).
There is no negative indexing (xs.at(-1) is a missing element); the last
element is xs.at(xs.length() - 1), which needs a handler or a guard.

== NO SUBSCRIPT OPERATOR
`xs[1]` is not an element access: `[` written right after a name starts the
explicit type arguments of a call
(`std::ffi::c::null[std::ffi::c::Void]()`), so the parser expects a type and
reports SURFACE SYNTAX ("expected a type after "xs["").

@example reject
xs = [1, 2, 3]
xs[1]
@error SURFACE SYNTAX
@at 2:4
@caption There is no subscript: write xs.at(1) (after import std::list)
@end

@example accept
import std::list

fn last_or(xs: List[int], fallback: int) -> int:
    xs.get(xs.length() - 1, fallback)

[[4, 5, 6].last_or(0), [].last_or(0)]
@value [6, 0]
@end

@topic task.number-text
title: Numbers to and from text
summary: std::int::to_string(n) and std::int::from_string(s) convert an Int's decimal text; std::float64::round rounds a Float64; std::decimal_rounded::to_string(x) shows a DecimalRounded value.
index: concepts stdlib
alias: integer to string; int to string; number to text; to_string; format number; itoa; tostring; string of a number; string to int; parse int; parse number; atoi; text to number; round a float; nearest integer; decimal to string; format money
keywords: convert, string, text, decimal, format, parse, round, to_string, from_string
related: stdlib.module.std::int stdlib.std::int::to_string stdlib.std::int::from_string stdlib.std::float64::round stdlib.std::float64::truncate stdlib.std::decimal_rounded::to_string concept.decimal-rounded concept.integers capability.string-formatting
docs: NUMERIC-TEXT-BOUNDARY-MILESTONE-1.md

== HOW
    import std::int
    import std::float64
    import std::decimal_rounded

    n.to_string()                       Int -> its decimal text
    std::int::from_string(s)            text -> Int, errors InvalidIntegerText
    x.round()                           Float64 -> nearest Int, ties to even
    x.truncate()                        Float64 -> Int toward zero
    d.to_string()                       DecimalRounded{minDecimalsPreserved: N}
                                        -> its decimal, exactly N places

Every conversion is explicit: nothing turns a number into text by itself,
`+` never concatenates, and there is no format string
(capability.string-formatting). An Int's text is the canonical one --
digits, no leading zero, "-" when negative -- and from_string accepts
exactly that, so the two are inverses. round and DecimalRounded share one
rule, round half to even, on a value's exact binary value. A DecimalRounded
value shows the decimal its type guarantees, never its representation's
digits. There is no Float64 to String conversion beyond a program's printed
result, no other base, and no parsing of a Float64 or a DecimalRounded
value.

@example accept
import std::decimal_rounded
import std::float64
import std::int
import std::str

fn label(n: int, x: Float64, best: DecimalRounded{minDecimalsPreserved: 1}) -> str:
    "floor ".concat(n.to_string()).concat(" at ").concat(x.round().to_string()).concat(" best ").concat(best.to_string())

fn seed(s: str) -> int:
    std::int::from_string(s):
        on InvalidIntegerText:
            0

[label(12, 266.5, 9.95), label(-3, 2.5, 6.0), seed("42"), seed("4x")]
@value ["floor 12 at 266 best 10.0", "floor -3 at 2 best 6.0", 42, 0]
@caption Every number converted explicitly; the halves round to even
@end

